<GuideLayout slug="palm-beach-county-tree-removal-permit">
  <p class="note">
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> in Palm Beach County, the city your property is in usually decides. In unincorporated
    areas the county's land development code applies and a Tree Removal and Replacement permit is required for
    non-residential sites and for buffer and common-area trees. HOAs add their own rules, and a dangerous tree on a
    single-family home can be exempt under state law when a certified arborist documents the risk.
  </p>

  <h2>Cities set most of the rules</h2>
  <p>
    Palm Beach County has many municipalities, each with its own tree ordinance:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/areas/boca-raton-tree-removal/">Boca Raton</a>'s environmental office says its vegetation chapter does not apply to trees within the property lines of single-family and owner-occupied two-family homes, so no city permit is required there. Other property types, historic and coastal areas and HOAs differ.</li>
    <li><a href="/areas/west-palm-beach-tree-removal/">West Palm Beach</a> has its own tree protection rules.</li>
    <li><a href="/areas/delray-beach-tree-removal/">Delray Beach</a> requires an application to remove many trees and palms, with a size threshold set by the city.</li>
    <li><a href="/areas/boynton-beach-tree-removal/">Boynton Beach</a> treats single-family lots, commercial sites and recorded landscape buffers differently.</li>
    <li><a href="/areas/jupiter-tree-removal/">Jupiter</a> often leaves single-family lots in planned communities to the homeowners' association.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Unincorporated Palm Beach County</h2>
  <p>
    In unincorporated areas the county's Unified Land Development Code applies. The county's zoning FAQ says all
    non-residential sites need a Tree Removal and Replacement permit to remove trees, and that in single-family
    developments a permit is needed to remove perimeter buffer and common-area trees. It does not say a permit is needed
    for every tree on a single-family lot, so ask the county before you cut. The county's Environmental Resources
    Management department handles vegetation removal permits (561-233-2400).
  </p>
  <p>
    Some species, such as Australian pine, Brazilian pepper and melaleuca, are classed as prohibited, and must be removed
    from development sites.
  </p>

  <h2>HOAs and communities</h2>
  <p>
    Many Palm Beach County neighborhoods are managed by an association that must approve removing or replacing a tree,
    independent of the city or county. Ask your HOA first.
  </p>

  <h2>The state exemption for dangerous trees</h2>
  <p>
    Under Florida Statute 163.045, a local government may not require a permit, fee or replacement tree for removing a
    tree on residential property (a detached single-family home) if the owner has documentation from an ISA certified
    arborist or a Florida licensed landscape architect that the tree poses an unacceptable risk. Keep the signed report.
    See <a href="/certified-arborist/">arborist assessments</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens if you skip the permit</h2>
  <p>
    Violations can lead to a Notice of Violation, fines and replacement requirements. The cost is usually higher than the
    permit, so check first.
  </p>

  <h2>Next step</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/contact-us/">Request a free estimate</a> and ask the professional what is usually required for your
    address. For rules across South Florida, read our <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">permit guide</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="text-base text-text-muted">
    This is general information, not legal advice, and rules change. Details checked in October 2026 against the
    <a href="https://discover.pbc.gov/pzb/FAQPages/Zoning-Landscape.aspx">Palm Beach County zoning landscape FAQ</a> and
    <a href="https://m.flsenate.gov/Statutes/163.045">Florida Statute 163.045</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
